   "date": "2020-04-24T15:49:31Z",
   "subject": "Pooling sessions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word;line-break:after-white-space\">Hello,<div><br></div><div>I have a large multi-session dataset (see screenshot) where I want to calculate sigma and dectection by session. Some sessions were quite sparsely sampled, and in that case I’d like to be able to pool these to share estimates. </div><div><br></div><div>There are 42 sessions, and a few of them I would like to pool. For example 29:33 were all in the same place, just in different years, and sampling was low intensity (thus so were recaptures). I would like to pool these together and only estimate a single detection and sigma value across these, while estimating independent estimates across the other sessions. </div><div><br></div><div>I feel like this must be easy, but I went down a rabbit hole of complex coding and decided to step back and ask the experts!</div><div><br></div><div>Cheers,</div><div>Clayton<br><div><br></div><div><img src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/oscr_package/attach/2644ca757e773/Screen%20Shot%202020-04-24%20at%209.45.02%20AM.png?part=0.1.1&amp;view=1\"><br><div>\r\n<div>--<br>Clayton Lamb, PhD <br>Postdoctoral Researcher<br>Universities of British Columbia &amp; Montana<br>e: <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ctl...@ualberta.ca</a><br>p: <a href=\"tel:(778)%20215-0334\" value=\"+17782150334\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">778-215-0334</a><br>t: @ClaytonTLamb<br>w: <a href=\"http://www.lamb-eco-research.ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.lamb-eco-research.ca&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792492117832000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2SPcr2AebgfBXD8O-eErWx\">www.lamb-eco-research.ca</a></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<br></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "FC4B-IhcAwAJ",
   "from": "Chris Sutherland",
   "author": "58a62321f08f30f8",
   "date": "2020-04-27T19:38:29Z",
   "subject": "RE: [oscr] Pooling sessions",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Hi Clayton,<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">We did some of ‘structured pooling’ in our <a href=\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-26847-x\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-018-26847-x&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792492117837000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2B_3TpOV4fSnyCRi-WuUFq\">mink paper</a> and our <a href=\"https://esj-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/1438-390X.12045\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://esj-journals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/1438-390X.12045&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792492117837000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2Ttlbb00haNEzVLkhS2NB8\">cheetah paper</a>. If I understand correctly, I think the following fabricated example is one approach.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Let’s say we have 6 session, 3 in protected areas, 3 outside. The inference objective is to test whether density, sigma, or detection vary by protection status. So, without any covariates, we can easily estimate constant parameters (no variation, D~1) or, using the built in model ‘~Session’, we can estimate session-specific parameters. NB: this works for p0, Sig, and D. But, we can also so a lot more through creative covariate generation. Here is a way to add covariates to pool the protected and not protected sessions (‘Prot’ ‘uProt’, respectively).<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><u><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Density:<u></u><u></u></span></u></b></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">#6 sessions: 1-3 are Prot, 4-6 are uProt<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"># ssDF is the ssDF object<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"> <u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">prot &lt;- rep(c(‘Prot’,’uProt’),each=3)<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">for(i in 1:6){<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">  ssDF[[i]]$prot &lt;- factor(prot[i],levels= c(‘Prot’,’uProt’))<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">}<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-indent:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Now you can specify ~prot in the density model and estimate an intercept (Prot) and the coefficient (contract) for not protected. Likewise, you could add continuous variable to estimate effects (latitude for example).<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><u><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Sigma:<u></u><u></u></span></u></b></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">#6 sessions: 1-3 are Prot, 4-6 are uProt<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"># scrFrame is the scrFrame object<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"># sigCovs is a dataframe with nSession rows, and nCovaraite columns.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"># i.e., sigCovs is like a session covariate data frame<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"> <u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\">scrFrame$sigCovs$prot &lt;- factor(rep(c(‘Prot’,’uProt’),each=3)) #adding the new covariate here<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-indent:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"text-indent:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Now you can specify ~prot in the sigma model and estimate an intercept (Prot) and the coefficient (contract) for not protected. Likewise, you could add continuous variable to estimate effects (latitude for example).<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><u><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Baseline encounter (p0/g0):<u></u><u></u></span></u></b></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\" style=\"margin-left:.5in\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">In this case, following the same idea, you can add covariates to the tdf (add a column called prot, giving all traps is a session the same value). Now you can specify ~prot in the detection model and estimate an intercept (Prot) and the coefficient (contract) for not protected. Likewise, you could add continuous variable to estimate effects (latitude for example).<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Using this approach gives a huge amount of flexibility in terms of ‘structured pooling’ beyond the simple ‘all or session’ approach that the majority of applications default to.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Hope this helps, but if I’m way off the mark, let me know and I can have another go.<u></u><u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\">Chris<u></u><u></u></span></p></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div lang=\"EN-US\" link=\"blue\" vlink=\"purple\"><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Lucida Console&quot;;color:#385723\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Garamond&quot;,serif;color:black\"><u></u> <u></u></span></p><div><div style=\"border:none;border-top:solid #e1e1e1 1.0pt;padding:3.0pt 0in 0in 0in\"><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\">From:</span></b><span style=\"font-size:11.0pt;font-family:&quot;Calibri&quot;,sans-serif\"> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a> [mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a>] <b>On Behalf Of </b>Clayton Lamb<br><b>Sent:</b> Friday, April 24, 2020 11:49 AM<br><b>To:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">oscr_p...@googlegroups.com</a><br><b>Subject:</b> [oscr] Pooling sessions<u></u><u></u></span></p></div></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Hello,<u></u><u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I have a large multi-session dataset (see screenshot) where I want to calculate sigma and dectection by session. Some sessions were quite sparsely sampled, and in that case I’d like to be able to pool these to share estimates. <u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">There are 42 sessions, and a few of them I would like to pool. For example 29:33 were all in the same place, just in different years, and sampling was low intensity (thus so were recaptures). I would like to pool these together and only estimate a single detection and sigma value across these, while estimating independent estimates across the other sessions. <u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">I feel like this must be easy, but I went down a rabbit hole of complex coding and decided to step back and ask the experts!<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Cheers,<u></u><u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">Clayton<u></u><u></u></p><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><img border=\"0\" width=\"1368\" height=\"336\" src=\"https://groups.google.com/group/oscr_package/attach/35c88f8012e14/image001.png?part=0.1\"><u></u><u></u></p><div><div><p class=\"MsoNormal\">--<br>Clayton Lamb, PhD <br>Postdoctoral Researcher<br>Universities of British Columbia &amp; Montana<br>e: <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ctl...@ualberta.ca</a><br>p: <a href=\"tel:(778)%20215-0334\" value=\"+17782150334\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\">778-215-0334</a><br>t: @ClaytonTLamb<br>w: <a href=\"http://www.lamb-eco-research.ca\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.lamb-eco-research.ca&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792492117837000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0hrxwkUT6Vv7IIfLmetOto\">www.lamb-eco-research.ca</a><u></u><u></u></p></div></div><p class=\"MsoNormal\"><u></u> <u></u></p></div></div></div></div>"
